  <name><text>Ch03_P02_Ex1_Product_of_odd_numbers</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Conjecture: the product of two odd numbers is always a multiple of \(3\).</p>
<p>Give a counterexample.</p>
<div>First number: [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</div>
<div>Second number: [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</div>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>The conjecture claims that every product of two odd numbers is a multiple of \(3\). A counterexample is therefore a pair of odd numbers whose product is <em>not</em> a multiple of \(3\).</p>
<p>Take \(1\) and \(5\). Both are odd, and \(1\times 5=5\), which is not a multiple of \(3\). So the product of two odd numbers is not always a multiple of \(3\), and the conjecture is false. (Others: \(5\times 7=35\), \(1\times 1=1\), \((-1)\times 5=-5\).)</p>
<p>A pair such as \(3\) and \(5\) does not work: \(3\times 5=15\) is a multiple of \(3\). Because \(3\) is prime, a product is a multiple of \(3\) exactly when one of its factors is, so a counterexample needs two odd numbers that are not multiples of \(3\).</p>
<p>A true statement about such products is: the product of two odd numbers is always odd.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
